  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crimes scan src/api/state.ts --format json` returns exactly one `singular_plural_type_mismatch` finding — `crime_00024` — covering two declarations. Severity `low`, confidence `0.7`, effort `quick`, `blast_radius: 0`.\n\nSummary, verbatim:\n\n> \"2 declarations whose plural form disagrees with the annotated type's array shape.\"\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - \"plural name, singular type: `users: User` @L24\"\n> - \"singular name, array type: `invoice: Invoice[]` @L27\"\n> - \"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\"\n\n`fix_shape`, verbatim:\n\n> \"make the name agree with the type — `users` for array, `user` for scalar\"\n\n---\n\n## Declaration 1 — `users: User` @ L24\n\n```ts\n// singular_plural_type_mismatch: plural name, singular type.\nexport const users: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n```\n\n**Recommendation: (a) rename — the type carries the intended meaning.**\n\nThe tiebreaker is the initializer, which the detector doesn't quote but which settles it: `{ id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" }` is one object literal with one id and one email. Both the annotation *and* the value agree this is a single user; only the name disagrees, and it disagrees alone. When two of three signals point one way, the odd one out is the typo. Changing the type instead (`users: User[]`) would mean rewriting a working value to chase a name — inventing a collection that nothing in the file ever asked for.\n\n**The rename is not free.** Line 33 already declares the obvious target:\n\n```ts\nexport const user: User = { id: \"u_2\", email: \"x@y.z\" };\n```\n\nSo `users` → `user` collides in-module. Two ways out, and you have to pick deliberately:\n\n- If `u_1` and `u_2` are meant to be *the same* concept, L24 is a duplicate of L33 — delete it and keep one.\n- If they're genuinely distinct roles, rename to something that says which user: `currentUser`, `requestUser`, `primaryUser`. A qualified singular is the fix; a bare `user` is a merge you may not have meant.\n\n## Declaration 2 — `invoice: Invoice[]` @ L27\n\n```ts\n// singular_plural_type_mismatch: singular name, array type.\nexport const invoice: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\n**Recommendation: (a) rename — again the type carries the intended meaning, but for a different reason.**\n\nHere the initializer is `[]`, which is uninformative about cardinality — an empty array is the natural seed for a collection and also the natural placeholder for nothing. So the value abstains and the decision rests on the annotation alone. `Invoice[]` is explicit and deliberate; someone typed the brackets. Prefer the side that was stated over the side that was named casually.\n\n**Same collision.** Line 34 already has:\n\n```ts\nexport const invoices: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\n`invoice` → `invoices` is a byte-for-byte duplicate of L34, including the initializer. This one is not ambiguous the way L24 was: **delete L27.** There's nothing to preserve.\n\n---\n\n## Two things to check before you apply either fix\n\n**Don't reshape the types to resolve this.** Option (b) is a trap in this file specifically. `crime_00032` and `crime_00033` flag `User` and `Invoice` in `src/api/state.ts` as **Contract Split-Brain** against declarations of the same names in `src/billing.ts`:\n\n> \"A: `Invoice` (interface) — src/api/state.ts:9, 2 field(s)\"\n> \"B: `Invoice` (interface) — src/billing.ts:19, 11 field(s)\"\n\nThe local `Invoice` is a 2-field stub disagreeing with an 11-field sibling on `createdAt`, `currency`, `discountCents`, `subtotalCents`, `taxCents`, and `userId`. Editing that local type to satisfy a naming complaint deepens a `high`-severity contract split to settle a `low`-severity naming one. Rename the bindings; leave the interfaces to the contract-drift fix.\n\n**The finding set is a floor, not a ceiling.** The evidence discloses its own limit — \"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\". A declaration typed `UserList`, `Array<User>`, or `readonly User[]` would not appear in this scan. This file happens to contain none, so the two findings are complete *here*, but don't read a clean scan elsewhere as proof of no mismatches.\n\n**Blast radius is genuinely zero.** The finding reports `blast_radius_direct_importers: 0`, and grepping the fixture confirms nothing imports this module — consistent with the file's own header, \"This file is fixture material — do not import it from real code.\" Both renames are local-only. That said, this file is a detector fixture: the negative controls at L30–34 exist to prove the detector *doesn't* fire on correct names, so editing it changes what the scanner reports about itself.",
